# Caddyfile - Caddy's configuration file.
# Docs: https://caddyserver.com/docs/caddyfile
#
# This file lives at /var/packages/caddy/var/Caddyfile on the NAS (a copy
# of this template is installed there once, on first install, and is never
# overwritten again - edit it freely).
#
# After editing, either restart the Caddy package from Package Center, or
# apply changes without downtime by running:
#   caddy reload --config /var/packages/caddy/var/Caddyfile
#
# The default site below listens on :8880 so it doesn't collide with
# anything else on the NAS out of the box.
#
# To run on a low port, the caddy binary needs the cap_net_bind_service
# capability. The package grants this itself, automatically, right before
# every service start (DSM's installer runs postinst/postupgrade without
# the privilege needed to set it there, so it's done in service_prestart
# instead - the same point spksrc's own dnscrypt-proxy package relies on
# to bind port 53). If it ever doesn't take - check the service log for a
# "setcap failed" warning - grant it yourself:
# 	setcap 'cap_net_bind_service=+ep' /var/packages/caddy/target/bin/caddy
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Freeing ports 80/443 on the NAS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DSM itself often already holds these, even with Web Station stopped - its
# Login Portal redirects plain http://<nas>/ to the DSM management UI on
# :5000/:5001. To free them:
#   Control Panel -> Login Portal -> Advanced -> Applications tab
#     -> edit the "DSM" entry, change or disable its port 80/443 binding
#   Control Panel -> Network -> DSM Settings
#     -> uncheck "Automatically redirect HTTP connections to HTTPS" if set
# (On older DSM 6.x, look for "Application Portal" instead of "Login
# Portal" - the menu was renamed in DSM 7.)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Automatic HTTPS via Let's Encrypt
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Caddy gets you free, auto-renewing HTTPS just by naming a real domain
# instead of a bare port. To serve your own domain:
#   1. Point a DNS A record (and AAAA, if you have IPv6) for your domain or
#      subdomain at your public IP.
#   2. Forward ports 80 and 443 (TCP) from your router/firewall to this
#      NAS's LAN IP. Port 80 is needed too, even though visitors end up on
#      443 - Caddy uses it for the ACME HTTP challenge and to redirect
#      plain HTTP to HTTPS.
#   3. Make sure nothing else on the NAS is still holding 80/443 (see
#      above), then replace the :8880 site block below with your real
#      domain, e.g.:
#
#        example.your-domain.com {
#            respond "Caddy is running"
#        }
#
#   4. Restart the Caddy package (or `caddy reload`, see above). Caddy
#      fetches and renews the certificate automatically from then on - no
#      further action needed. Certificates are stored under
#      /var/packages/caddy/var/data, so they survive package restarts and
#      upgrades.
#
# Optional: set a contact address for Let's Encrypt's renewal notices with
# a global options block at the very top of this file:
#
#   {
#       email you@example.com
#   }

:8880 {
	respond "Caddy is running"
}

# Example reverse proxy to another service on the NAS:
# example.your-nas.synology.me {
# 	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5000
# }
